<p>Total ≈ 27 agent-days.</p>
<h3>7.3 Order</h3>
<pre><code>Wave 0:  T0
Wave 1:  A  B  D  E          (parallel; each tests against T0 fixtures)
Wave 2:  C  F                (F mocks search until C lands)
Wave 3:  G  H
Wave 4:  I  J                (parallel; H&#39;s IPC is frozen in T0, so I/J can start against a fake bridge during wave 3)
Wave 5:  K  (+ Reviewer)
</code></pre>
<p>Calendar: with 4–5 agents in parallel, about 2–2.5 weeks of build + about 1 week for integration, review and 재영님&#39;s UI feedback → Phase 1 in roughly 3–4 weeks. Later phases (rough): Phase 2 3–4 weeks, Phase 3 4–6 weeks, Phase 4 6–10 weeks (server, auth, billing, security review).</p>
<h3>7.4 Rules for every swarm brief</h3>
<ul>
<li>Touch only the owned paths; changes to contracts go back to the Lead, never edited locally.</li>
<li><code>refs/</code> is study-only; no copied code.</li>
<li>Tests use the T0 fixtures; each task runs only its own tests; full gates stay with the Reviewer/Lead.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8. Acceptance for Phase 1</h2>
<ul>
<li>Registering a Project creates its space with Tasks/Outputs/Decisions/Journal within one owner cycle; deleting <code>place/index</code> and <code>place/export</code> and restarting rebuilds both with identical search results.</li>
<li>An agent task session that finishes produces exactly one Outputs entry (re-delivery does not duplicate) and one journal line, both attributed to that agent and session id.</li>
<li>A <code>place create</code> from a delegated worker in Project X lands in X&#39;s space; the same call targeting another project&#39;s space fails with <code>FORBIDDEN_SPACE</code>; a body containing <code>sk-…</code> fails with <code>SECRET_DETECTED</code> and nothing is persisted.</li>
<li>Two processes writing the same object concurrently converge to the same state after compaction.</li>
<li>Board drag in the UI and a <code>place update props.status</code> from an agent at the same time both survive.</li>
<li>Markdown export → import fixture round-trip is lossless for the v1 node set.</li>
</ul>
<h2>9. Risks and open decisions</h2>
<p>Open decisions for 재영님</p>
<ul>
<li>D1 Body format: <code>Y.XmlFragment</code> with the Tiptap schema (recommended: Notion-like blocks, direct Tiptap collaboration, Hocuspocus-ready) vs markdown in <code>Y.Text</code> (simpler, Obsidian-like, weaker block features).</li>
<li>D2 Index store: PostgreSQL schema <code>place</code> (recommended: already used by memory and schedules, embeddings later) vs <code>node:sqlite</code> (lighter, works without the PG instance).</li>
<li>D3 Who may write to Common: only the user and Mixy (recommended) vs every bot vs per-bot permission.</li>
<li>D4 Auto-capture scope: every agent task session and routine result (recommended, with dedupe) vs only sessions that produced files vs opt-in per bot; and whether sessions get a one-line Place pointer in context.</li>
<li>D5 Location and devices: one Place per data dir (recommended) — confirm that PC and phone remote access share the PC&#39;s Place until Phase 4.</li>
<li>D6 Sessions outside any Project (no <code>.mixdog/project.id</code>): writes go to Common&#39;s Inbox, to the bot&#39;s space, or are refused (recommended: bot space for bots, refuse for workers).</li>
<li>D7 Open-source boundary and plan: Place client stays in this Apache-2.0 repo; is the Phase 4 sync/sharing server closed and separate (and hosted on <code>apps/relay</code> or a new service)? What is free vs the $4.99 plan?</li>
<li>D8 Retention: keep everything forever, or archive Outputs older than N days from search.</li>
</ul>
<p>Risks</p>
<ul>
<li>Headless Tiptap/markdown in Node may need DOM shims → checked in T0; fallback is D1&#39;s alternative.</li>
<li>Yjs v14 is in RC (<code>refs/yjs</code>); we pin 13.x and plan a migration later.</li>
<li>Memory/disk growth from many small docs → lazy body loading, compaction, snapshot size monitoring.</li>
<li>Junk accumulation despite caps → dedupe + quotas + per-bot stats in the UI; revisit after a week of real use.</li>
<li>Prompt injection between bots via shared Common content → data wrapper in tool results, Common writes restricted (D3).</li>
<li>Secret-pattern false positives blocking legitimate content → precise patterns + clear error so the agent can rephrase; user UI shows a warning, not a silent drop.</li>
<li>Multi-process ownership (daemon vs headless CLI) → append-only update files are safe without the lock; only compaction/index need ownership.</li>
<li>Name clash with existing <code>AgentChatPlace</code> in the renderer → contract naming rule in §4.2.</li>
</ul>
